1) How does ping work when PC1 pings PC2 for the first time:

    - when PC1 wants to ping PC2 for the first, it will first check the destination IP address,
    - it needs to determine whether the destination is in the same subnet or in a different subnet

    1. Check the subnet:
    --------------------
    - if the destination IP is in the same subnet, PC1 knows that it can communicate directly
      with PC2. it dose not need to send the packet to the default gateway

    2. Check the ARP cache:
    -----------------------
    - next, PC1 will check its ARP cache to see whether the MAC address corresponding to the
      destination IP is already available
    "since this is the first ping, normally there will not be an ARP entry for the first IP address"

    3. Send an ARP request:
    -----------------------
    - because the MAC address is not available, PC1 will generate an ARP request
    - the ARP request is a broadcast frame because PC1 does not know which device owns the destination IP address
    - the destination MAC address of the ARP request will be FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF, which is the broadcast MAC address

    4. What the switch does:
    ------------------------
    - when the switch receives this broadcast frame, it will forward the frame to all the ports in the same VLAN
      except the port on which it received the frame
    - as a result, every device connected to that VLAN will receive the ARP request

    5. PC2 replies:
    ---------------
    - every device will check the target IP address in the ARP request,
    - Only PC2 will recognize that the requested IP address belongs to it.
    - then PC2 will send an ARP reply back to PC1.
    - Unlike the ARP request, the ARP replay is a unicast frame because PC2 already knows PC1's MAC address from teh received request

    6. Update the ARP cache:
    ------------------------
    - after receiving the ARP replay, PC1 will update it's ARP cache by storing the mapping between the destination IP address and its MAC address

    7. Send ping:
    -------------
    - once the MAC address is available, PC1 will create the ICMP Echo request, which is the
     actual ping packet,
    - the packet will be encapsulated into an Ethernet frame PC2's MAC address as the destination,
      and the switch will forward it only to PC2

    8. Receive the replay:
    ----------------------
    - after receiving the ICMP Echo request, PC2 will process it and send an ICMP Echo replay back to PC1,
    - the switch will forward the replay to PC1, and the ping will be successful.

    Closing Line:
    =============
    "so, during the first ping, the ARP process happens before the ICMP packet is sent,
    form the second ping onwards, since the ARP entry is already present in the cache,
    PC1 can send the ICMP packet directly without performing another ARP broadcast,
    unless the ARP cache entry has expired"


